Async code becomes misleading when execution order is mistaken for completion order.
Press enter or click to view image in full size

JavaScript code can look perfectly sequential while its asynchronous work finishes in a completely different order.
A function starts a database operation, moves to the next line, triggers another side effect, and returns. Reading from top to bottom makes the workflow feel ordered.
But starting work is not the same as waiting for it to finish.
The calls begin in source order. Their completion is controlled by promises, timing, network responses, and external systems.
That distinction is where async code becomes misleading.
The common explanation is that someone forgot await. That is technically correct, but it does not reach the deeper problem.
The real mistake is allowing unfinished work to exist without deciding who owns it.
Who waits for the result? Who handles rejection? Can later work begin before it finishes? Is retrying safe? What does the function mean when it reports success?
Async code becomes dangerous when those questions are answered accidentally by syntax.
Starting Work Was Not the Same as Completing It
Consider a backend registration workflow:
async function createUser(input: CreateUserInput) {
saveUser(input);
sendWelcomeEmail(input.email);
return { success: true };
}The code visually suggests a sequence:

• Save the user.

• Send the welcome email.

• Report success.
That is only the invocation order.
If saveUser returns a promise, JavaScript starts the operation and immediately moves to sendWelcomeEmail. The email may begin before the user exists in the database. The function may return while both operations are still running.
Nothing in the source order guarantees that either operation has completed.
This often appears harmless during development because local dependencies respond quickly. The database usually finishes first, so timing makes the code look correct.
The weakness appears when the environment becomes less predictable.
The database slows down. The email provider responds first. The process exits after returning the response. One promise rejects after the request has already been reported as successful.
The visual sequence represents invocation order, not completion order.
If email delivery depends on the saved user, the dependency must be explicit:
async function createUser(input: CreateUserInput) {
const user = await saveUser(input);
await sendWelcomeEmail(user.email);
return user;
}Now the email operation does not begin until the user has been saved.
That establishes order. It does not define the complete workflow.
If the user is persisted but email delivery fails, should account creation fail? Should the email be retried? Should the user be returned with a pending notification state?
await makes the dependency visible. The surrounding design still needs to explain what partial success means.
Returning { accepted: true } could also be valid, but only if the contract explicitly means the work was queued or started rather than completed. It becomes misleading when callers interpret acceptance as proof that every side effect finished successfully.
The Variable Looked Finished. The Value Was Still a Promise
A missing await can change more than timing. It can change the value and contract of the current function.
Consider this version:
async function createUser(input: CreateUserInput) {
const user = userRepository.save(input);
await emailService.sendWelcomeEmail(user);
return user;
}The variable is named user, but it contains Promise<User>.
The variable name tells one story. The value tells another.
Strong TypeScript definitions should catch the mismatch. Broad types, weak wrappers, or JavaScript-only code can allow the promise to travel further before the mistake becomes visible.
The correction may be to await the promise locally:
const user = await userRepository.save(input);But local waiting is not always required.
Returning the promise can be valid:
function createUser(input: CreateUserInput): Promise<User> {
return userRepository.save(input);
}Here, ownership is transferred clearly to the caller. The caller decides when and how to await the result.
The mistake is not that every promise must be awaited where it is created.
The mistake is allowing ownership to remain accidental.
A promise should normally be awaited, returned, coordinated with other promises, or intentionally detached with an explicit failure path. Otherwise, unfinished work disappears from the visible control flow.
The Same Ownership Mistake Appeared Inside forEach
The ownership problem becomes less obvious when the code already contains an await.
This pattern looks sequential:
users.forEach(async (user) => {
await sendWelcomeEmail(user);
});
console.log("All emails sent");The callback waits for sendWelcomeEmail inside its own execution.
The parent operation does not wait for the callback promises.
forEach ignores the promises returned by its callback. It invokes every callback and finishes. The log may run while every email is still pending.
The code contains await, but the parent operation does not own the awaited work.
If emails must be sent sequentially, the order should be explicit:
for (const user of users) {
await sendWelcomeEmail(user);
}
console.log("All emails sent");If the operations are independent and safe to run concurrently, collect the promises:
await Promise.all(
users.map((user) => sendWelcomeEmail(user)),
);
console.log("All emails sent");These approaches express different contracts.
The for...of loop waits for one operation before starting the next. That may be appropriate when ordering matters or the provider enforces strict rate limits.
Promise.all starts the operations together and waits for the group. If one promise rejects, the combined promise rejects, but the other operations already started may continue.
That matters when the work has side effects.
If ten emails are submitted and one promise rejects, several others may still be delivered. Retrying the entire batch without tracking individual outcomes can create duplicates.
When partial results matter, Promise.allSettled, bounded concurrency, or explicit per-item tracking may better match the workflow.
The choice depends on whether the operation requires ordering, concurrency limits, complete success, or visibility into partial failure.
Request Order Was Not Completion Order
The same mistake appears when multiple calls compete to control one result.
Consider frontend search:
async function search(query: string) {
const results = await api.search(query);
setResults(results);
}The function is correct when viewed in isolation.
The problem appears when calls overlap.
A user searches for java, starting one request. A moment later, the user searches for javascript, starting another.
The second request may finish first.
The interface displays the correct javascript results. Then the older java request completes and replaces them with stale data.
Every individual promise resolved successfully. The combined behavior is still wrong.
The issue is not a missing await. Completion order has been allowed to determine ownership of the screen.
One solution is to let only the latest request update the interface:
let latestRequestId = 0;
async function search(query: string) {
const requestId = ++latestRequestId;
const results = await api.search(query);
if (requestId !== latestRequestId) {
return;
}
setResults(results);
}Another option is cancellation through AbortController when the client and API support it.
Cancellation still needs careful interpretation. Aborting the client request can stop the client from waiting for or accepting the result. It does not always prove that the remote server stopped processing the request.
That distinction is minor for a read-only search. It becomes critical when the request performs a side effect.
Product behavior determines which result still matters when it finishes. Async syntax cannot choose that policy for us.
Fire-and-Forget Promises Hid Failure
Not every promise should delay the current response.
Analytics, cache invalidation, low-priority notifications, and telemetry may not belong on the critical path.
That can lead to code like this:
async function updateProfile(input: ProfileInput) {
const profile = await profileRepository.update(input);
analytics.track("profile_updated", {
profileId: profile.id,
});
return profile;
}This may be an intentional performance decision.
It is still incomplete unless the detached operation has a failure path.
If analytics.track rejects, who observes it? Does it become an unhandled rejection? Is the event safe to lose?
Intentional detachment should look intentional:
void analytics
.track("profile_updated", {
profileId: profile.id,
})
.catch((error) => {
logger.warn("Analytics event failed", {
profileId: profile.id,
error,
});
});The void operator is mainly a signal to readers and lint rules that the promise is deliberately not awaited. The .catch is what establishes the failure path.
That may be sufficient for low-value telemetry.
It is not sufficient for work that must survive a process crash.
Audit records, payment events, and important customer notifications may require a durable queue, transactional outbox, or persistent job record. An in-memory promise belongs to the current process. Once that process disappears, it can no longer own the work.
The important distinction is not awaited versus unawaited.
It is owned versus abandoned.
Retries Repeated Side Effects the Code Never Tracked
Completion ownership becomes more dangerous when a workflow crosses independent systems.
Consider this version:
async function createUser(input: CreateUserInput) {
const user = await userRepository.save(input);
await emailService.sendWelcomeEmail(user);
return user;
}The order is explicit. The user is saved before the email is sent.
Now suppose the email provider accepts the message, but the connection closes before the application receives confirmation.
The promise rejects.
The caller sees failure and retries createUser.
Several outcomes are possible:

• the database rejects a duplicate user;

• another user record is created;

• the welcome email is sent twice;

• the retry stops at persistence and never investigates the first email;

• the application reports failure even though both operations completed.
await guarantees that the function waits. It does not guarantee atomicity.
The database and email provider do not share one transaction. A rejection means the caller did not receive a usable success result. It does not always prove that the remote side effect failed.
Async code starts lying when one rejected promise is allowed to imply that nothing succeeded.
A safer design may separate account creation from email delivery.
The database transaction could create the user and a durable outbox record. A worker could process that record using a stable operation identifier. Email delivery could then be retried without repeating user creation.
That design adds infrastructure and operational responsibility. It may be excessive for low-risk workflows.
The machinery becomes justified when the side effect is important, externally visible, difficult to reverse, or expensive to duplicate.
Retries require a model of what may already have completed, not merely another try block around asynchronous code.
The Better Model Was Ownership of Unfinished Work
I used to review async code mainly by searching for missing await keywords.
I now ask who owns each unfinished operation.
Every promise needs one of a few explicit outcomes:

• the current function waits for it;

• the caller receives ownership;

• a group coordinates it;

• a background path handles its failure;

• a durable system takes responsibility beyond the current process.
The correct choice depends on the consequence of unfinished work.
